{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310632,"nonce":"ZfIsfpm1YphvZaDoBsrHsu1gYPE=","title":"FIXED: BLADE cache pollution made `restore_cached_maps` seed `import_constructor_name_map` with the *saving* session's renumbered ids. ...","message":"With multi-var parser support enabled (RD `parse_block_expr` splicing `var a = 1, b = 2;` into two sibling `Var` block elements), `test_stringbuf_stringtools` reliably SIGILLed (exit 132) under the haxe regression suite. Reproduced only after ~100 prior tests populated `.rayzor/blade/cache/`. Did not reproduce under lldb. Crash was pre-main with no program output.\n\n\n`restore_cached_maps` at [compilation.rs:3108-3113](compiler/src/compilation.rs#L3108-L3113) seeds `import_constructor_name_map` directly from `BladeCachedFunctionEntry.func_id`:\n\n```rust\nif entry.is_constructor {\n    self.import_constructor_name_map\n        .insert(entry.class_name.clone(), IrFunctionId(entry.func_id));\n    continue;\n}\n```\n\n`entry.func_id` is the renumbered id from the *saving* session — encoded as `100_000 + saving_import_index*10_000 + local_id`. The saving session may have loaded StringBuf at import index 10 (id fn200007), while the current session loads it at index 2 (id fn120007).\n\nThe renumber pass in `renumber_and_push_import_mir` rewrites map entries through `id_map`, but `id_map` only covers *the module being renumbered*. When StringBuf renumbers, its local id_map is {fn0…fn7 → fn120000…fn120007}; `id_map.get(fn200007)` returns None, so the cached `StringBuf → fn200007` entry in `import_constructor_name_map` is left alone.\n\nWhen the user file then lowers `new StringBuf()`, `constructor_name_map.get(\"StringBuf\")` returns the stale fn200007. The CallDirect targets an id that doesn't exist in the merged module → SIGILL on first dispatch.\n\nThe cross-module *CallDirect* paths in cached MIR were already handled correctly via the name-first resolution in renumber + fixup_stale_cross_module_refs. The constructor map was the one map keyed by *class name* (not by source func_id), so the existing fixup didn't touch it.\n\n\nSame source file ⇒ same MIR shape, so only ids differ. Dumping `external_function_names` from both cached `StringTools.blade` files made the import-index drift visible:\n\n```\nbaseline: fn120007: StringBuf.new   (StringBuf at index 2)\nvariant:  fn200007: StringBuf.new   (StringBuf at index 10)\n```\n\nThen `RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR=1` on the final merged module showed exactly one unresolved id: `call fn200007($1)` in `@main`. Constructor lookup, not call-site rewriting.\n\n\n[compilation.rs](compiler/src/compilation.rs) — added `fixup_stale_constructor_ids` that runs alongside `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` after all imports are loaded. Rewrites each `import_constructor_name_map[class_name]` by looking up `\u003cclass_name\u003e.new` in `stdlib_function_name_map` (which carries the current session's real ids):\n\n```rust\nfn fixup_stale_constructor_ids(\u0026mut self) {\n    let map_snapshot = self.stdlib_function_name_map.clone();\n    for (class_name, func_id) in self.import_constructor_name_map.iter_mut() {\n        let qualified = format!(\"{}.new\", class_name);\n        if let Some(\u0026current_id) = map_snapshot.get(\u0026qualified) {\n            if *func_id != current_id { *func_id = current_id; }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe clone is a snapshot to avoid borrowing `self` twice. The map is small (~100 entries) so the clone cost is negligible.\n\n\n- `parse_block_expr` in [parser/src/rd/expr.rs](parser/src/rd/expr.rs) re-enabled multi-var splice — single `var a = 1, b = 2;` parses into two sibling Var block elements. Int64.hx no longer falls through to the legacy nom parser; only one RD warning remains in the suite (vs the prior multi-var dance).\n- `rm -rf .rayzor \u0026\u0026 bash run_haxe_tests.sh` → 127/128 PASS (the 1 fail is the documented [[bugs-test-exception-flake]] re-running 2/3 PASS, unrelated to this fix).\n- `test_stringbuf_stringtools` PASSes deterministically with cumulative cache state.\n\n\nWhen you add a cross-module mapping that's keyed by *name* but stores a *func_id* (the constructor map, the future field-access map, the future static-method map), it has to be name-resolved through `stdlib_function_name_map` after all imports are loaded — the integer ids in the cache are not stable across sessions. Don't trust that renumbering alone is sufficient; renumbering only knows about each module's local id_map.\n\nRelated: [[bugs-forward-ref-stub-unresolved]] (the analogous fix for cross-module CallDirect via ext_names), [[bugs-osr-analysis-multivar]] (the OSR analysis that ruled out tier transitions before the cache-diff angle found this).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_int64_multivar_stub_replace` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}